1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:09,787
Here's your SRT file converted to clean plain text with all timestamps, numbering, and table formatting removed:

2
00:00:09,737 --> 00:00:15,362
**PHASE 1 — FOUNDATION**

3
00:00:15,362 --> 00:00:21,362
Welcome to Phase 1 of building a production-grade Financial AI Agent.

4
00:00:21,362 --> 00:00:24,081
This is the foundation.

5
00:00:24,081 --> 00:00:26,096
The bedrock.

6
00:00:26,096 --> 00:00:32,909
Everything else we build over the coming hours and phases rests on what we create right now.

7
00:00:32,909 --> 00:00:47,659
By the end of this two-hour session, you'll have a complete, working Python project with a modern structure, comprehensive configuration, a production-ready database layer, and everything tested and verified.

8
00:00:47,659 --> 00:00:50,690
Let me show you the big picture.

9
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:56,799
Data comes from SEC EDGAR — tens of thousands of financial filings.

10
00:00:56,799 --> 00:01:03,503
We ingest them, parse them, chunk them, and store them in a vector database.

11
00:01:03,503 --> 00:01:11,206
Then when you ask a question, we retrieve the most relevant chunks and generate an answer using an LLM.

12
00:01:11,206 --> 00:01:14,174
But this is just Phase 1.

13
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:22,878
In later phases, we'll add multi-agent orchestration, real-time data streaming, and predictive analytics.

14
00:01:22,878 --> 00:01:28,299
For now, we're building the foundation that makes all of that possible.

15
00:01:28,299 --> 00:01:31,565
Let's start with the prerequisites.

16
00:01:31,565 --> 00:01:39,049
You need Python 3.11 or higher, Docker, Docker Compose, and Git.

17
00:01:39,049 --> 00:01:45,690
If you don't have these, pause the video, get them installed, and come right back.

18
00:01:45,690 --> 00:01:50,565
You do not need an OpenAI API key for this phase.

19
00:01:50,565 --> 00:01:56,565
We use local services exclusively until we're ready to connect to the cloud.

20
00:01:56,565 --> 00:02:04,221
This means no cost, no API limits, and you can run everything on your local machine.

21
00:02:04,221 --> 00:02:08,346
Let's begin by creating the project directory.

22
00:02:08,346 --> 00:02:13,393
Open your terminal and run: mkdir financial-rag-agent

23
00:02:13,393 --> 00:02:19,253
Then change into that directory with: cd financial-rag-agent.

24
00:02:19,253 --> 00:02:23,956
And initialize Git: git init

25
00:02:23,956 --> 00:02:28,409
Now, here's where many tutorials go wrong.

26
00:02:28,409 --> 00:02:31,487
They just throw files everywhere.

27
00:02:31,487 --> 00:02:42,721
We're going to use what's called the "src layout" — a modern standard for Python packages that prevents import conflicts and makes your code actually installable.

28
00:02:42,721 --> 00:02:50,456
The src layout is the industry standard because it forces you to be intentional about what you're importing.

29
00:02:50,456 --> 00:02:56,174
You import from 'financial_rag', not from the root directory.

30
00:02:56,174 --> 00:03:02,424
This ensures your package works when installed — not just when you're running it locally.

31
00:03:02,424 --> 00:03:09,003
Think of it like this: a flat layout is like having all your files on your desktop.

32
00:03:09,003 --> 00:03:12,799
It works when you're the only one using it.

33
00:03:12,799 --> 00:03:19,159
But as soon as you have multiple projects or multiple people, it becomes chaos.

34
00:03:19,159 --> 00:03:24,690
The src layout is like having a well-organized filing system.

35
00:03:24,690 --> 00:03:27,424
Everything has its place.

36
00:03:27,424 --> 00:03:31,018
You always know where to find things.

37
00:03:31,018 --> 00:03:37,378
And when someone else looks at your project, they immediately understand the structure.

38
00:03:37,378 --> 00:03:42,049
Here's the command to scaffold all directories at once.

39
00:03:42,049 --> 00:03:50,112
Run this in your terminal: mkdir -p src/financial_rag/{config,storage}

40
00:03:50,112 --> 00:03:59,643
Let me break down what each of these directories does, because understanding this structure is the key to understanding the entire system.

41
00:03:59,643 --> 00:04:04,737
config' holds all settings and environment configuration.

42
00:04:04,737 --> 00:04:10,221
This is the single source of truth for how your application behaves.

43
00:04:10,221 --> 00:04:13,659
Every other component reads from here.

44
00:04:13,659 --> 00:04:21,393
If you want to change the database host, the embedding model, or the logging level, you change it here.

45
00:04:21,393 --> 00:04:27,721
storage' contains database clients, cache clients, and repositories.

46
00:04:27,721 --> 00:04:36,456
This is your data access layer — everything that talks to PostgreSQL, Redis, or any other data store.

47
00:04:36,456 --> 00:04:41,549
This is where the actual data lives and how we interact with it.

48
00:04:41,549 --> 00:04:45,799
We also need Python package marker files.

49
00:04:45,799 --> 00:04:57,690
These are empty __init__.py files that tell Python, "Hey, this directory is a package you can import from.

50
00:04:57,690 --> 00:05:04,237
Without these, Python would not recognize these folders as importable packages.

51
00:05:04,237 --> 00:05:11,284
It's a small detail that trips up beginners all the time — so let's get it right from the start.

52
00:05:11,284 --> 00:05:28,393
Run this command to create __init__.py files in every directory: find src -type d -exec touch {}/__init__.py \;

53
00:05:28,393 --> 00:05:36,128
Now, let's examine the most important file in any modern Python project: pyproject.toml.

54
00:05:36,128 --> 00:05:49,362
This single file replaces setup.py, requirements.txt, setup.cfg, flake8.ini, mypy.ini, and pytest.ini.

55
00:05:49,684 --> 00:05:52,699
Think about what that means.

56
00:05:52,649 --> 00:05:58,446
Instead of managing six different configuration files, you have one.

57
00:05:58,446 --> 00:06:01,446
One file to rule them all.

58
00:06:01,446 --> 00:06:09,212
This simplifies project management enormously and reduces the cognitive load of setting up a new project.

59
00:06:09,212 --> 00:06:13,337
At the top, we specify the build system.

60
00:06:13,337 --> 00:06:19,774
We use hatchling, which is lightweight and doesn't need a MANIFEST.in file.

61
00:06:19,774 --> 00:06:22,930
It's the modern Python standard.

62
00:06:22,930 --> 00:06:31,508
Under the project section, we define the package name, version, description, and Python version requirement.

63
00:06:31,508 --> 00:06:36,712
This is the metadata that makes your package findable and installable.

64
00:06:36,712 --> 00:06:41,821
The dependencies section lists all production dependencies.

65
00:06:41,821 --> 00:06:46,680
These are the packages your application actually needs to run.

66
00:06:46,680 --> 00:06:52,337
Every dependency here is carefully chosen for a specific purpose.

67
00:06:52,337 --> 00:06:56,383
FastAPI is our web framework.

68
00:06:56,383 --> 00:07:01,493
It's modern, fast, and built on Python type hints.

69
00:07:01,493 --> 00:07:06,758
Uvicorn is the ASGI server that runs FastAPI.

70
00:07:06,758 --> 00:07:12,258
Pydantic handles configuration validation and data parsing.

71
00:07:12,258 --> 00:07:16,696
Pydantic-settings reads from .env files.

72
00:07:16,696 --> 00:07:22,696
SQLAlchemy 2.0 provides async database access.

73
00:07:22,696 --> 00:07:27,290
asyncpg is the PostgreSQL driver.

74
00:07:27,290 --> 00:07:32,212
pgvector adds vector search to PostgreSQL.

75
00:07:32,212 --> 00:07:36,227
Alembic handles database migrations.

76
00:07:36,227 --> 00:07:39,071
Redis provides caching.

77
00:07:39,071 --> 00:07:43,524
OpenAI is for LLM and embedding integration.

78
00:07:43,524 --> 00:07:46,649
tiktoken counts tokens.

79
00:07:46,649 --> 00:07:51,555
httpx is our HTTP client.

80
00:07:51,555 --> 00:07:55,477
BeautifulSoup4 parses HTML.

81
00:07:55,477 --> 00:07:59,196
structlog provides structured logging.

82
00:07:59,196 --> 00:08:02,915
tenacity handles retry logic.

83
00:08:02,915 --> 00:08:07,962
The dev dependencies are separate from production dependencies.

84
00:08:07,962 --> 00:08:14,462
These are not installed in production — they're only used during development and testing.

85
00:08:14,462 --> 00:08:17,899
pytest is the testing framework.

86
00:08:17,899 --> 00:08:22,165
pytest-asyncio enables async tests.

87
00:08:22,165 --> 00:08:25,712
pytest-cov measures test coverage.

88
00:08:25,712 --> 00:08:29,758
mypy is the static type checker.

89
00:08:29,758 --> 00:08:35,977
ruff is the fast linter that replaces black, isort, and flake8.

90
00:08:35,977 --> 00:08:39,540
pre-commit runs hooks before commits.

91
00:08:39,540 --> 00:08:43,758
This tells hatchling where to find our package.

92
00:08:43,758 --> 00:08:48,790
The packages are in src/financial_rag.

93
00:08:48,790 --> 00:08:52,712
This is where the build system looks for the code.

94
00:08:52,712 --> 00:08:56,696
Now let's look at the pytest configuration.

95
00:08:56,696 --> 00:09:03,446
asyncio_mode is set to 'auto' — this enables async test support.

96
00:09:03,446 --> 00:09:09,493
testpaths is set to 'tests' — this is where pytest looks for tests.

97
00:09:09,493 --> 00:09:16,071
pythonpath includes 'src' — this allows imports to work correctly.

98
00:09:16,071 --> 00:09:21,758
Without this, pytest wouldn't be able to find your application code.

99
00:09:21,758 --> 00:09:25,852
This is a common pitfall that I want you to avoid.

100
00:09:25,852 --> 00:09:31,790
python_files matches test_*.py.

101
00:09:31,790 --> 00:09:35,962
python_classes matches Test*.

102
00:09:35,962 --> 00:09:40,883
python_functions matches test_*.

103
00:09:40,883 --> 00:09:44,837
These patterns tell pytest what to look for.

104
00:09:44,837 --> 00:09:52,008
We define markers: 'unit' for unit tests and 'integration' for integration tests.

105
00:09:52,008 --> 00:09:59,368
Integration tests require external services like PostgreSQL and Redis to be running.

106
00:09:59,368 --> 00:10:04,149
This separation is crucial for fast test feedback.

107
00:10:04,149 --> 00:10:09,133
You can run just unit tests with: pytest -m unit.

108
00:10:09,133 --> 00:10:14,383
Or just integration tests with: pytest -m integration.

109
00:10:14,383 --> 00:10:21,008
This flexibility is incredibly useful in CI/CD pipelines.

110
00:10:21,008 --> 00:10:27,008
**END OF PHASE 1 — FOUNDATION**

111
00:10:27,008 --> 00:10:40,696
**✅ Done!** The SRT has been converted to clean plain text with all timestamps, numbering, and table formatting removed.

